  completeExplanation: 'Class 6 Science Chapter 12 introduces young learners to the cosmos through observation and historical context. Starting with Yangdol and Dorjay in Ladakh\'s dark skies, students explore the difference between stars (which generate their own light through nuclear energy and twinkle due to atmospheric refraction) and planets (which shine steadily by reflecting sunlight). The sky is mapped into 88 official constellations by the IAU. Key constellations include Orion the Hunter, whose three belt stars point eastward to Sirius in Canis Major (the brightest star in the night sky), and Taurus the Bull containing Aldebaran (Rohinī) and Pleiades (Krittikā). The Big Dipper (Saptarishi in Ursa Major) features two pointer stars that lead directly to the Pole Star (Polaris / Dhruva Tārā in Ursa Minor), which appears stationary above Earth\'s North Pole and serves as a reliable navigational compass for the Northern Hemisphere. The chapter explores the Solar System centered around the Sun (a star 150 million km or 1 Astronomical Unit away, ~100 times Earth\'s diameter). Eight planets revolve around the Sun: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars (inner rocky planets), followed by Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune (outer gas/ice giants with ring systems and numerous moons). Venus is highlighted as the hottest planet due to a dense carbon dioxide greenhouse atmosphere, and Pluto is clarified as a dwarf planet reclassified in 2006. Earth\'s Moon revolves in 27 days at a distance of 3,84,000 km, exhibiting an airless, cratered landscape from meteorite impacts. Between Mars and Jupiter lies the Asteroid Belt, while icy comets (Dhūmaketu) from outer space develop glowing vapor tails when approaching the Sun (e.g. Halley\'s Comet every 76 years). The chapter proudly details India\'s space triumphs by ISRO: Chandrayaan-1 discovering water on the Moon, and Chandrayaan-3\'s historic soft-landing near the lunar South Pole on 23 August 2023, celebrated annually as National Space Day. It honors ancient astronomers like Aryabhata (who established Earth\'s daily axial rotation and scientific shadow theory of eclipses), Varahamihira, and Bhaskara. Finally, it presents the Milky Way Galaxy (Ākāśha Gaṅgā), exoplanets, and the Hanle Dark Sky Reserve in Ladakh, encouraging pristine sky watching with 30-minute dark adaptation.',
